<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Görlitz</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zgorzelec</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Görlitz is a town on the Lusatian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Neisse</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s easternmost town. It prospered on the cloth trade and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-regia">Via Regia</a> road and was a leading member of the Six Towns League of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a> from 1346. The mystic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakob-bohme">Jakob Böhme</a> lived there. In 1945 the river became the Polish border, and the eastern part became Zgorzelec.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gorlitz.jpg" alt="Görlitz" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Kraft · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MKr361484_St._Peter_und_Paul_(G%C3%B6rlitz).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Görlitz lies on the Lusatian Neisse and is the largest town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a> and the easternmost town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Slavs settled the area in the Early Middle Ages and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorbs">Sorbs</a>, a Slavic people, founded the town, as its name shows: it probably derives from a Slavic word for burned land, referring to a method of clearing land. Opposite stands the Polish town of Zgorzelec, which was the eastern part of Görlitz until 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Görlitz, as Goreliz, was first mentioned in a document of Henry IV in 1071. It stood on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-regia">Via Regia</a>, the old trade route linking western and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the settlement gradually became a town in the 13th century. It passed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and the Piast Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawor">Jawor</a> before falling back to Bohemia in 1329. From 1346 it was a wealthy member of the Lusatian League with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamenz">Kamenz</a>, Lubań, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lobau">Löbau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zittau">Zittau</a>, and it was the capital of a duchy from 1377 to 1396.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> arrived in the early 1520s and the town became almost wholly Lutheran. In 1547 Ferdinand I, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-bohemia">King of Bohemia</a>, punished it for disobedience in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-war">Schmalkaldic War</a>, taking away customs and toll rights. In 1635, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a> was ceded to the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> gave the town to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815, and it was administered within Silesia until 1945.</p><p>In March 1933 the SA set up the Leschwitz concentration camp in the town, dissolved that August. During the Kristallnacht of November 1938 the synagogue was set on fire but survived, because firefighters ignored the order not to put out the blaze, and it is the only synagogue in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> to have outlasted Nazi rule. The number of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> fell from 567 in 1925 to 134 in 1939. The town was spared major destruction in the war, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oder-neisse-line">Oder–Neisse line</a> of 1945 divided it, and the eastern bank became Zgorzelec in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Görlitz has about 56,000 inhabitants and, with Zgorzelec, forms the Euro City of Görlitz/Zgorzelec. The border was open to visa-free travel from 1972 until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> closed it in 1980, and movement became unrestricted again when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> joined the Schengen area on 20 December 2007. The old town has Gothic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a>, Neoclassical and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/art-nouveau">Art Nouveau</a> buildings, among them the Schönhof, one of the oldest civic Renaissance buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and the Silesian Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Görlitz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1346 Bautzen, <b>Görlitz</b>, Zittau, Kamenz, Löbau and Lauban, towns of the Margraviate of Upper Lusatia, then a land of the Kingdom of Bohemia, formed a league to suppress robber knights and protect their trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/lusatian-league">Lusatian League</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1346 Bautzen, <b>Görlitz</b>, Zittau, Lauban, Kamenz and Löbau formed the Six Towns League, protecting trade routes.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-upper-lusatia">Margraviate of Upper Lusatia</a></li></ul></section>
